    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 282043\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n143 PM PDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Chances for showers and thunderstorms continue through Tuesday \n  along with cooler temperatures.\n\n* Stronger storms across the Colorado River Valley and northwestern \n  Arizona may produce flash flooding, small hail, and strong wind \n  gusts. \n\n* Mild weather and warming temperatures are expected during the\n  second half of the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through next weekend.\n\nSatellite imagery shows low pressure centered in southern Nye\nCounty this afternoon drifting southward. To the east of the \ntrough axis, showers and thunderstorms are moving to the northeast\nacross eastern San Bernardino, Clark, and Lincoln counties. There\nare also a few storms moving southward behind the trough in\nnorthern Inyo County. The moisture source for all this convective\nactivity is the remnants of former Tropical Cyclone Odalys. \nWraparound precipitation will linger through Tuesday over the \neastern half of the CWA, but will be more scattered than today. \n18Z sounding shows some good moisture at the edge of the moisture\ngradient. Being on the edge of the moisture gradient brings more \nthunder and less opportunity for flooding rain except in areas \nwhere stronger cells train over the same locations. The greater \nflash flood threat remains to the east, mainly over Mohave County \nand portions of Lincoln County. A flood watch remains in effect \nfor most of Mohave County through Tuesday.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, a drying and warming trend will begin as the\ntrough moves out and a ridge of high pressure begins to build \nover the desert southwest. This ridge will control the weather\nthrough the weekend, bringing clear skies and temperatures 5 to \n10 degrees above normal by the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast\nPackage...Elevated southeasterly winds will veer to the southwest\nearly in the forecast period, with gusts up to around 20KT. \nThunderstorms are expected to develop over the higher terrain \nsurrounding the Valley, with potential for impacts at the terminal\nduring the mid- to late afternoon. Erratic gusty outflow winds, \nlightning, and brief reductions in ceilings/visibility due to \nrainfall are possible with storms that move over the terminal. \nVicinity thunderstorms will persist until 12Z Tuesday morning, \nwith lingering vicinity showers thereafter. Outside of convection,\nceilings will be SCT-BKN around 10-12kft, with low confidence in \nthe wind forecast through early Tuesday morning due to potential \noutflow boundaries. However, confidence in wind direction \nincreases thereafter as winds shift to the northwest and north, \nincreasing with gusts to 20-25KT expected late Tuesday morning \nthrough the afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds across the\nregion shift to the south and southwest this afternoon, with \ngusts to 20-25KT common. One exception is KBIH, where gusty \nnorthwesterly to northerly winds will prevail through the forecast\nperiod, and another is KDAG, where winds maintain a westerly \ndirection with some subtle variability. The primary aviation \nconcern will be showers and thunderstorms across southern Nevada, \nfar southeastern California, and northwestern Arizona These storms\nare developing at issuance, with vicinity thunderstorms mentioned\nfor Las Vegas Valley and Colorado River Valley terminals through \n09-12Z Tuesday, and vicinity showers persisting thereafter. \nErratic gusty outflow winds, lightning, and ceiling/visibility \nreductions due to heavy rain can be expected if these storms \nimpact area terminals. The wind forecast in the presence of \nconvection remains uncertain, however, a northwesterly to \nnortherly wind shift with gusts to 20-25KT is expected late in the\nforecast period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
